WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:07.637 line:92%,end size:91%
A karyotype tells us which chromosomes a cell has, how
many copies there are, and whether their structure has
changed.

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:10.549 line:92%,end size:91%
These changes can inform a diagnosis.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:15.660 line:92%,end size:91%
When a living cell divides, can we see where an
abnormal chromosome count comes from?

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.800 line:92%,end size:91%
We lack a general way to label chromosome identities
through mitosis.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.716 line:92%,end size:91%
ChromoWorld brings two streams together. Fixed cells
provide the reference identities.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:31.515 line:92%,end size:91%
ChromoPix identifies them, and ChromoLift builds a
three-dimensional reference atlas.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:37.700 line:92%,end size:91%
ChromoPose separates live instances. Tracking links
them. ChromoID matches their shapes.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:45.685 line:92%,end size:91%
G-banding gives models two reliable cues in fixed
spreads: distinctive stripes and familiar shapes
produced by preparation.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:52.500 line:92%,end size:91%
The cues change with preparation. Banding-trained
models lose accuracy on rehydrated chromosomes.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:58.073 line:92%,end size:91%
Changing the architecture barely helps. Training
rewarded cues tied to preparation.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:05.600 line:92%,end size:91%
FISH brings us closer. Rehydrated chromosomes lack
bands, but DNA probes can identify them.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.155 line:92%,end size:91%
Probe labels are precise, but sparse within a field.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:18.892 line:92%,end size:91%
ChromoPix learns from the labels and from paired views
of the same chromosome. One view keeps the shape while
suppressing fine texture.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:26.684 line:92%,end size:91%
Aligning their features rewards what survives that
change. A chromosome-class prompt lets the decoder
focus on one identity at a time.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:33.093 line:92%,end size:91%
Follow one tagged chromosome. We measure its shape,
then turn those measurements into a point.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:42.281 line:92%,end size:91%
Pull back to more than seven hundred thousand
chromosome instances. Each point has a shape, and its
color marks a chromosome class.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.760 line:92%,end size:91%
The class arrangements are similar across both
preparations. Could those shape relationships help in
living cells?

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:56.800 line:92%,end size:91%
Whole-cell projections can collapse identities. Better
views recover only part of the order.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:05.866 line:92%,end size:91%
ChromoPose segments chromosomes in three dimensions.
Tracking links the instances. ChromoPhy uses their
measured geometry to infer identity.

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:16.598 line:92%,end size:91%
A live chromosome provides its own training pair: its
projection and its measured three-dimensional shape,
including volume, elongation, and sphericity.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:26.375 line:92%,end size:91%
ChromoLift learns that mapping with flow matching. It
moves compact shape descriptors, so thousands of
labeled references can be lifted in batches.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:35.625 line:92%,end size:91%
The same shapes, three results. Direct projection mixes
identities. Optimal projection helps. ChromoPhy
restores a clearer order.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:41.635 line:92%,end size:91%
ChromoID matches measured live shapes to the atlas,
ranking candidate identities.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:46.835 line:92%,end size:91%
We checked predictions with fluorescent tags on
chromosomes three and eighteen.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:53.425 line:92%,end size:91%
We used only DNA to predict. Then we revealed
fluorescence to check positions.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:03:03.037 line:92%,end size:91%
In patient-derived tumor cells, imaging suggested that
one daughter received two copies of chromosome fourteen
and the other received four.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.881 line:92%,end size:91%
We isolated daughter cells and grew them into separate
clones.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.324 line:92%,end size:91%
Copy-number differences persisted across passages.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:21.690 line:92%,end size:91%
Higher-copy clones showed stronger AKT signaling and
greater sensitivity to capivasertib in the lab.

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:26.950 line:92%,end size:91%
The clinical team independently chose capivasertib with
endocrine therapy.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.677 line:92%,end size:91%
Liver target lesions shrank by nearly a third at eight
weeks, and by nearly half at eighteen.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:40.458 line:92%,end size:91%
Tumor markers and liver biochemistry improved. Daily
function improved too, with less liver pain and
fatigue.

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:47.360 line:92%,end size:91%
Reading chromosome identities in living cells lets us
ask what an unequal division sets in motion.
